Chapter: DiagPchK1
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Dev chapter NicAsde
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage
  cites Diag chapter DiagPch
  cites Diag chapter DiagNicRing
  cites Net chapter NetDriver

 The same PHY register `pch` reads at stage 10, read again AFTER the driver
 has written it, because those two readings answer different questions and
 only the second one is about the fix.

 `pch` runs before anything touches the part, so its 770.17 is the platform's
 power-up value. The K1 write happens inside `e1000-init`
 (`Kernel chapter E1000e`, `e1000-pch-prepare`), which in this ladder is
 reached only through `net-driver-bring-up` in `b3`. Nothing read the register
 after that, so a flight where traffic still did not flow could not separate a
 fix that was applied and did not help from a fix that was never applied at
 all. This stage is that separation and it is the whole of what it does.

 WHERE IT SITS IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT A MATTER OF TASTE. It must run after
 the writer and before `asde`. `asde` calls `na-bring-up-after`, which calls
 `na-phy-kick` (`Dev chapter NicAsde`), which writes BMCR reset; a PHY reset
 returns 770.17 to its NVM value, which is the documented reason the driver
 writes K1 after `e1000-phy-bring-up` and not before. A reading taken after
 `asde` therefore reports the NVM value on every run and would say the write
 never took even when it took. Moving this stage later re-opens the exact gap
 it was built to close.

 Not "after b3" -- after the writer. If a later ladder brings `e1000-init` up
 earlier, this stage follows it.

 The register, its two bits and their citation are `Diag chapter DiagPch`,
 which owns them; this chapter reads them through that chapter's own accessors
 rather than restating the numbers, so the pair cannot drift apart.

 THE BED MOVES THIS ROW IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the property `pch`'s
 kmrn row does not have and declares. `-i219` powers the part up at the
 campaign's condition (K1 enabled, `Giga_K1_disable` clear) and the driver's
 write lands, so the row reads `taken`. `-i219-mng-holds` leaves firmware
 holding the MNG bit, the semaphore acquisition cannot succeed, and MDIO is
 refused: the row reads `no-mdio`, off `taken`, from the same binary.

 `not-taken` is BOARD-ONLY and is declared rather than left to be discovered.
 It needs MDIO to answer while the write fails to stick, and the model has no
 knob for that: every bed arm either lets the write land or refuses the read.
 On metal it is the reading the stage exists for.

 We say:

Section: States

  dpk-states : List Text = ["taken", "not-taken", "not-pch", "no-mdio", "no-part", "rejected", "bar-bad"]

Section: Verdict

  dpk-verdict : Boolean, Integer -> Text
  dpk-verdict (pch) (k1) =
   if pch == False then "not-pch"
   else if k1 < 0 then "no-mdio"
   else if bit-and k1 dpch-k1-disable-bit /= 0 then "taken"
   else "not-taken"

  dpk-colour : Text -> Integer
  dpk-colour (state) =
   if state == "taken" then diag-col-good
   else if state == "not-taken" then diag-col-bad
   else if state == "no-mdio" then diag-col-warn
   else diag-col-dim

  dpk-reading : Text -> Text
  dpk-reading (state) =
   if state == "taken" then "the write is in the register, so a part that still does not pass traffic was not fixed by K1"
   else if state == "not-taken" then "MDIO answered and the disable bit is CLEAR, so the K1 step did not take and nothing has tested the theory"
   else if state == "no-mdio" then "the register did not answer, so this says nothing about the write either way"
   else if state == "not-pch" then "not an I219, so e1000-pch-prepare returns before the K1 step and there is no write to read back"
   else "no part"

Section: The Listen After The Write

 NO STAGE LISTENED AFTER THE K1 WRITE (reek, 2026-08-21): the order is
 nicinit, nicring, b3, pchk1, asde, and only b3's bring-up writes K1, so the
 board could never show a descriptor landing once K1 was disabled, and the
 campaign's claim had no row that could confirm it on metal. This listens on
 the PRODUCTION ring b3 bound, 1.2 s, through the driver's own receive path,
 with GPRC read before and after. GPRC clears on read, so the first read
 fences the window and the second counts only frames the MAC accepted inside
 it. The words are nicring's three, so a reader who has learned one row has
 learned the other: quiet (nothing counted, nothing seen), arrived-visible
 (the driver's poll returned a frame), arrived-invisible (the MAC counted a
 frame and neither the poll nor a DD bit on the ring shows it, which is the
 stall the K1 step exists to remove). skipped says b3 never bound the driver,
 which is every run with no peer in DIAG.CFG, and is not a fault.

 The fuel is a bound on a stuck clock and nothing else: nicsit measured
 32.6 ns per empty poll on the board and the bed is faster, so a 1.2 s window
 is some forty million polls, and a cap of twenty million was the bed's
 window for the first run of this (polls=20000000 on the row, 0.6 s). Two
 hundred million is five windows; a row reading that number is the clock
 not advancing, which b3 has already refused on by then.

  dpk-listen-ms : Integer = 1200
  dpk-listen-chunk : Integer = 2000
  dpk-listen-fuel : Integer = 200000000

  dpk-poll-chunk : Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read] Integer
  dpk-poll-chunk (n) (got) =
   if n <= 0 then got
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    f <- net-driver-recv-frame
    let seen = if list-length f > 0 then 1 else 0
    in let r = __heap-restore hp
    in dpk-poll-chunk (n - 1) (got + seen)
   end

  dpk-listen-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read] (Integer, Integer)
  dpk-listen-loop (start) (want) (frames) (polls) =
   if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then (frames, polls)
   else if polls >= dpk-listen-fuel then (frames, polls)
   else act
    g <- dpk-poll-chunk dpk-listen-chunk 0
    dpk-listen-loop start want (frames + g) (polls + dpk-listen-chunk)
   end

  dpk-listen-word : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpk-listen-word (frames) (gprc) =
   if frames > 0 then "arrived-visible"
   else if gprc > 0 then "arrived-invisible"
   else "quiet"

  dpk-listen : Integer -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Network.Read] Text
  dpk-listen (mmio) =
   if net-driver-active /= net-card-e1000 then "listen-after-k1 word=skipped (b3 did not bring the driver up, so there is no live ring)"
   else let started = hpet-start
   in let hz = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if hz <= 0 then "listen-after-k1 word=skipped (no HPET rate to budget the window)"
   else let ring = bit-and (peek-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-rx-ring) 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in let gprc0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-gprc
   in let rdh0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rdh
   in act
    r <- dpk-listen-loop hpet-ticks ((hz * dpk-listen-ms) / 1000) 0 0
    when r
     is (frames, polls) ->
      let gprc1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-gprc
      in let dd = nir-dd-count ring 0 0
      in "listen-after-k1 word=" & dpk-listen-word frames gprc1 & " frames=" & show frames & " gprc-before=" & show gprc0 & " gprc-after=" & show gprc1 & " dd=" & show dd & " rdh=" & show rdh0 & " polls=" & show polls & " ms=" & show dpk-listen-ms
   end

 The RDH read between the fence and the window is a read and nothing else on
 the part; nicring reads the same register on every flight. In the bed it is
 what opens the wire: codex-vm delivers held frames on an RDH read, an RCTL.EN
 write or an RDT write, and a window that only polls descriptors performs none
 of those, so the first run of the blocked arm read quiet with the armed frame
 still held and the instrument could not say no (L-FALSIF).

Section: Rows

  dpk-part : PciDevice -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Network.Read] DiagResult
  dpk-part (d) =
   if na-eligible d == False then diag-result "rejected" diag-col-warn ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " " & na-reject-line d] ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " " & na-reject-line d]
   else let v = e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d)
   in if v /= e1000-bar-ok then diag-result "bar-bad" diag-col-bad ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " verdict=" & na-verdict-text v] ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " verdict=" & na-verdict-text v]
   else let mmio = na-mmio-of d
   in let pch = e1000-pch-part (d.pci-device-id)
   in let k1 = dpch-paged mmio dpch-k1-page dpch-k1-reg
   in let extcnf = e1000-read mmio dpch-extcnf-ctrl
   in let state = dpk-verdict pch k1
   in let r1 = "k1-after 770.17=" & dpch-hex k1 & " giga-k1-dis=" & dpch-bit k1 dpch-k1-disable-bit & " k1-en=" & dpch-bit k1 dpch-k1-enable-bit
   in let r2 = "pchk1 " & dpch-bdf d & " devid=" & diag-hex4 (d.pci-device-id) & " pch-part=" & na-yn (if pch then 1 else 0) & " k1-step-shipped=" & na-yn (if e1000-pch-k1-required then 1 else 0)
   in let r3 = "extcnf 0x00F00=" & diag-hex8 extcnf & " swflag=" & na-yn (bit-and extcnf dpch-swflag-bit) & " (the driver releases it inside e1000-init, so held here is the driver's own leak)"
   in let r4 = dpk-reading state
   in act
    r5 <- dpk-listen mmio
    diag-result state (dpk-colour state) [r1, r5, r4] [r2, r1, r3, r5, r4]
   end

  dpk-run : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio, Network.Read] DiagResult
  dpk-run (c) =
   when e1000-find (c.dc-pci)
    is None -> diag-result "no-part" diag-col-dim ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"] ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"]
    is Just (d) -> dpk-part d
